Subject: [overlay 0024] visionOS VR — the rendered yaw comes from the pose, not the client sim

The R3 root-cause fix for "jittery in both eyes as I look left and right" (first headset
round, 1.0.11.3), gated on the same q2vr state block overlays 0022/0023 introduced, so iOS,
the macOS oracle, the visionOS 2D window and the visionOS 3D panel are byte-for-byte
unchanged.

THE MECHANISM. R2 composed the rendered yaw as "the head's pitch and roll, plus THE GAME'S
OWN VIEW YAW" — `yaw_relative` added `cl.refdef.viewangles[YAW]`, which in a live game is
`cl.predicted_angles`, i.e. `cl.viewangles + delta_angles`. `cl.viewangles` is written by
`CL_UpdateCmd`, which runs on the CLIENT SIM tick, not on every rendered frame: with
`cl_async 0` and a `cl_maxfps` below the compositor's ~90 Hz cadence, `CL_Frame` returns
without advancing the sim on a large fraction of VR frames (the same early-return the R2
overlay already had to work around for the stale-left/fresh-right eye pair). So on those
frames the head pose was FRESH and the yaw the frame rendered with was one to two ticks
OLD. The compositor reprojects against the pose that was PUBLISHED, so every such frame was
reprojected by the residual between the published yaw and the rendered yaw — and that
residual is non-zero only while the head is turning, which is exactly and only when the
jitter was seen. Pitch and roll were always written straight from the pose; neither shook.

THE FIX (guide 12.7, the donors' architecture). The rendered yaw is body yaw + head yaw,
both owned by the shell and both taken from the pair published THIS frame. The only
engine-side term left is `server_yaw` — `ps.pmove.delta_angles[YAW]`, the server's own
re-orientation — which is a STEP the server sets on a teleport or a spawn and is neither
predicted, lerped nor smoothed. `yaw_relative` now selects THAT term. The angles the client
SENDS keep coming from the same head pose one step earlier and may lag by a sim tick; that
is the server's business and no longer the camera's.

  * q2_vr_state_t gains an APPENDED R3 section (shared.h): server_yaw, render_yaw and
    yaw_from_pose. The last two are identity-not-intent fields — what the frame ACTUALLY
    rendered with — so an assertion and a device report compare against the frame rather
    than against what the code is believed to do.
  * client/view.c: the yaw composition, and the two identity fields written at the site.
    `game_yaw` is kept and is now DIAGNOSTIC ONLY: it is the client-simulated yaw, and the
    whole point of this patch is that the rendered basis no longer reads it.

Nothing else in the engine changes. The panel-aspect and eye-sizing work of the same round
lives entirely in the platform shell.

Apply: patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this
--- a/inc/shared/shared.h
+++ b/inc/shared/shared.h
@@ -1313,6 +1313,34 @@
